Power off the PC and disconnect the power cable.
Open the case and reseat the RAM modules, making sure both retaining clips lock into place.
If there are multiple RAM sticks, test one stick at a time to identify a potentially faulty module.
Run Windows Memory Diagnostic (mdsched.exe) or a more thorough tool such as MemTest86.
Check whether the PC restarts during the memory test or reports any memory errors.
Test the suspected RAM module in another compatible slot to distinguish a bad RAM stick from a faulty motherboard slot.
Return the working configuration and run a demanding game or benchmark for an extended period to confirm the PC remains stable.
